According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are different for function $g$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}g(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\Big(2g(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^-}g(x)=1}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^+}g(x)=3}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\Big(2g(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a multiple of a function is the multiple of the limit:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^-}\Big(2g(x)\Big)=2\cdot\lim_{x\to 0^-}g(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^+}\Big(2g(x)\Big)=2\cdot\lim_{x\to 0^+}g(x)
\end{align}$
